# 3.1: 16th for an hour, then 65th

Good news, and then the same slide again

Unit 3, talk 2

---

## What 3.1 contained

R1 to R3 of the climb, released so live rating could start accruing:

- **R1 client reliability:** learn the real tick allowance, cap round-end writes, warm up before the first tick
- **R2 full share against weak bots:** more full-power rules, a rammer response
- **R3 a gun for surfers:** decaying ratings, 9 to 12 features, a hybrid gun

---

## The first hour

- At 11:45 UTC on 2026-09-30, 3.1 was **16th** at 86.74 APS
- That was only its first 53 pairings
- The same 53 opponents still average 86.74 in the complete page, so nothing about them was lucky
- The first-hour rate would be rank 21 over a full pass

---

## Then the slide

| Hour (UTC) | Pairings | APS | Survival |
|---|---|---|---|
| 11:00 | 97 | 85.7 | 91.8 |
| 12:00 | 171 | 82.1 | 85.2 |
| 13:00 | 154 | 79.7 | 79.1 |
| 14:00 | 90 | 78.6 | 76.1 |

Mean opponent APS was 49.5 in every hour. It was not the opponent mix.

---

## The final standing

- 512 pairings, **81.46 APS**, survival 83.0%, rank **65th**
- Rank 30 that day needed 84.30 APS, rank 40 needed 83.11
- To reach the top 30, Hadur needed **+2.9 APS**
- Had every pairing been fought at the first hour's PBI, 3.1 would have 84.3 APS, exactly rank 30 (L-23)

---

## It was 3.0 again

| | 3.0 before / after | 3.1 first hour / from 13:20 |
|---|---|---|
| APS | 85.5 / 77.2 | 85.7 / 78.2 |
| Survival | 91.6 / 72.5 | 91.8 / 75.9 |

- Every band lost 6 to 8 APS
- Head to head on 213 common opponents, 3.1 was **+0.6** over 3.0: noise
- R1 had not fixed it (L-22)

---

## Trying to reproduce it (3.2, R4)

A client-conditions bench (BENCH-4) varied:

- a shared, never-wiped data directory
- CPU constant forced to 1.0 ms and 0.3 ms
- four background-load threads
- a data directory built like a real client's

Survival stayed **97 to 100%** every time. The drop to 71-77% did not appear (L-21).

---

## Shipping a fallback anyway

3.2 shipped what could cap the damage of an unknown cause:

- **RES-7:** after three faulting ticks, fire power 1.0 instead of holding fire forever
- **RES-8:** a small health record at battle end, so a client run is self-describing
- **BENCH-5:** a reader that turns a saved live page into the band and hour reports you saw here

---

## What it taught us

- A great first hour is a measurement of the early state, not of the robot
- Compare the same opponents across time to separate a slide from a change in the mix
- When a fix does not move the live number, say so (L-22 "refutes the hope")
- When the bench cannot reproduce a problem, the bench is missing something. That was the next question.

---

